opencode installs npm packages into the user's home at runtime. reify() is
the single place any of it happens; the callers are

  - the plugin loader, which runs on every start, unprompted, and installs
    @opencode-ai/plugin into ~/.config/opencode/node_modules;
  - the formatter and LSP integrations, which fetch prettier, biome,
    typescript-language-server and friends on demand;
  - the provider loader, for model SDKs that are not already bundled.

A distribution package must not fetch and execute code from a third party
registry. All three call sites already handle a failed install: the plugin
loader logs a warning, Npm.which falls back to Option.none, and Npm.add
surfaces InstallFailedError as an ordinary provider error. So refusing here
degrades rather than breaks, and a tool the user installed themselves is
still found on PATH.

OPENCODE_ALLOW_NPM_INSTALL=1 restores upstream behaviour.

--- a/packages/core/src/npm.ts
+++ b/packages/core/src/npm.ts
@@ -79,6 +79,12 @@
     const directory = (pkg: string) => path.join(global.cache, "packages", sanitize(pkg))
     const reify = (input: { dir: string; add?: string[] }) =>
       Effect.gen(function* () {
+        // openSUSE: do not download executable code from the npm registry at
+        // runtime. Every caller treats a failed install as a soft failure.
+        if (process.env["OPENCODE_ALLOW_NPM_INSTALL"] !== "1") {
+          yield* Effect.logDebug("npm install disabled by distribution policy", { dir: input.dir })
+          return yield* new InstallFailedError({ add: input.add ?? [], dir: input.dir })
+        }
         yield* flock.acquire(`npm-install:${input.dir}`)
         const { Arborist } = yield* Effect.promise(() => import("@npmcli/arborist"))
         const add = input.add ?? []
